Hi Michal,

> I'm creating a separate thread, mostly to cut off from the ongoing
> flamewar in the one started by JG Miller.  I agree that anniversaries
> are a good time for reflection so let's try to evaluate the current
> situation of Nethack and its community and what can be done to make
> things better (as opposed to concentrating on what's wrong with the
> Devteam).

that's a good idea, I think; thinking about what can be done is surely 
better to bring results than criticising the DevTeam for whatever 
reasons they have to be how they are.

> 1. Work on the current codebase, adding new features and fixing
> things, basically continuing what slashem and sporkhack had started.

That's of course the easy way and likely to give results able to show 
back much earlier.  On the other hand, according to the few times I 
dived into NetHack's source, it is not really like something I'd want to 
work on extensively.

> 2. Break Nethack into pieces and assemble it into a modern OS project,
> not only code wise, but in general.  By that I mean community
> oriented, with a bugtracker, repository, beta releases, etc.  Code
> wise that would mean translating into a modern programming language
> like C++, Java, Python, D, etc (most likely object-oriented).

I'm pretty sure D and Java is out if you want to find some people here 
working with you; and I guess Python also (at least C++ would be the 
only one of those languages I'd want to work with for this project). 
Regarding C++, it's my opinion that this can make things much easier 
than plain C.  But if you really want to get some hackers onto your 
boat, my feeling is that staying with C would be the best choice.

Do you mean 2 to be something like "write a new rogue-like based on 
NetHack gameplay from scratch" or instead "start with NetHack sources, 
but bear in mind that the first projects are to rewrite it piece-wise"?

